Q = [
 ('reactor', 'Why does a naquadah generator need shielding around it?',
  'A running generator gives off radiation, so the casing is built to shield everyone nearby.',
  ['The shell keeps the fuel from freezing, since naquadah stops reacting once it drops below room temperature.',
   'The casing blocks stray light, because naquadah decays faster whenever visible light falls on the fuel cell.',
   'Shielding is mostly for show, since the whole reaction is already sealed inside the fuel cell itself.']),
 ('reactor', 'What happens if a naquadah generator is pushed into an overload?',
  'It can feed back on itself and release a huge burst of energy in half a minute.',
  ['It shuts itself down at once and cools for a few minutes before a technician can safely restart it.',
   'It vents the extra energy as a harmless flash of light that fades within a second or so of the surge.',
   'It simply stops making power, and the fuel stays inert until the core is swapped out for a fresh one.']),
 ('reactor', 'Why can a naquadah generator keep a gate running when its own power fails?',
  'A gate stores energy from many sources, so a generator can feed it just as lightning or another supply can.',
  ['Only naquadah power can wake a gate, because the ring recognizes its own fuel and rejects any other source.',
   'The generator carries a copy of the gate\'s control crystal, which restarts its memory and dialing from scratch.',
   'A generator draws its power out of the wormhole itself, so it can only work once a connection is already open.']),
 ('reactor', 'Why is a small amount of refined naquadah so useful in the field?',
  'A little refined naquadah gives a lot of clean energy, so a compact generator can run a team\'s equipment.',
  ['Naquadah burns so slowly that a single pellet will run a lamp for a century without any need for shielding.',
   'It is found almost everywhere and costs next to nothing, so teams just pour a bucket of it into the core.',
   'It absorbs heat from the surrounding air, so a field generator stays cold and needs no maintenance at all.']),
 ('reactor', 'Why would a much larger gate be a power problem?',
  'The energy to hold a stable wormhole climbs steeply with gate size, so a giant gate needs vastly more power.',
  ['Larger gates leak more heat, so their extra power draw is really just the cooling load of the whole ring.',
   'A larger ring spins more slowly while dialing, and the extra time spent turning is what burns the power.',
   'The wormhole gets wider, but the energy stays the same because it is set only by the distance traveled.']),
 ('dial', 'Why do gate addresses need to be corrected over time?',
  'Stars drift slowly, so a gate\'s coordinates change over thousands of years and gates exchange updates to correct it.',
  ['Gate rings wear down as they spin, so the symbols have to be recalibrated by hand every few hundred years.',
   'Planets shift in their orbits around their suns, so every address has to be recalculated at the start of each year.',
   'Each gate is reassigned a new address whenever a team visits it, which stops unwanted visitors from following.']),
 ('dial', 'Why do a few far-off worlds need more than the usual seven symbols?',
  'The network uses 39 symbols, and a very distant destination needs one more symbol plus extra energy to reach.',
  ['Distant worlds use a longer address because the symbols repeat, and more of them are needed to stay unique.',
   'The seventh chevron weakens with distance, so extra symbols are added to keep the whole signal clear to the far gate.',
   'Far worlds use the same seven symbols but are dialed twice, once for each leg of the trip across space.']),
 ('dial', 'What does the gate do to a traveler crossing the event horizon?',
  'It turns the traveler into energy, and the receiving gate reassembles them in a fraction of a second.',
  ['It shrinks the traveler down to a tiny size, then restores the original size once they arrive on the far side.',
   'It opens a narrow tunnel through solid space, so the traveler simply walks the whole distance within seconds.',
   'It freezes the traveler for the length of the trip, then lets them go once they reach the receiving gate.']),
 ('dial', 'Why did Earth need a dialing computer at all?',
  'Earth\'s gate had no dial-home device, so the Air Force built a computer to stand in for one and select the address.',
  ['The Ancients built the computer into the gate, and the SGC only had to find it and switch it on again.',
   'The gate cannot dial without a human operator, so the computer is only there to check the operator\'s identity.',
   'The computer is a security measure that lets only a short list of authorized addresses ever connect to the base.']),
 ('dial', 'Why can a wormhole not stay open forever under normal conditions?',
  'Normally a connection stays open about 38 minutes at most, though a big power surge can change that.',
  ['The event horizon thins out as it ages, and the gate shuts it for safety after only a few minutes of use.',
   'The gate runs out of symbols to hold the lock, since each chevron only lasts a certain number of seconds.',
   'It can stay open indefinitely, and the only real limit is how long the dialing computer can hold the link.']),
 ('sec', 'What is the iris made of, and why?',
  'It began as titanium and was later replaced with a trinium and titanium composite that stands up to extreme heat.',
  ['It is solid naquadah, because only that metal can survive the energy of an event horizon for any length of time.',
   'It is lead-lined ceramic, chosen because it absorbs the radiation that arrives through the wormhole each time.',
   'It is plain steel, since the iris never actually touches the event horizon and so takes no real stress at all.']),
 ('sec', 'Why can the gate room still talk to a team when the iris is closed?',
  'Radio waves pass through a closed iris and the wormhole, so the team and the gate room can keep talking.',
  ['The iris opens a small hatch for communications while the rest of the barrier stays closed against matter.',
   'A thin cable is run through the iris before it closes, and it carries the voice link in both directions.',
   'Teams cannot talk with the iris closed, which is why the iris is always opened first before anything is said.']),
 ('sec', 'Why is a team kept ready at the ramp when the iris opens under fire?',
  'If something follows the team through, or the iris must stay open, armed personnel have to be ready to stop it.',
  ['The team is there to catch the returning travelers, since arrival can be unsteady for the first few seconds.',
   'The ramp has to be cleared of loose equipment before anyone comes through, and the team handles that task.',
   'It is a ceremonial guard that greets visitors, and it has no real part in the defense of the base itself.']),
 ('sec', 'Why is iris control limited to authorized people?',
  'Opening the iris lets anything through, so the control is tied to a reader that only authorized staff can use.',
  ['The iris is delicate, and only a trained technician is allowed to operate it without damaging the plates.',
   'The iris is wired into the base power supply, and a wrong command by anyone else could black out the facility.',
   'It has no restrictions, because the iris closes by itself whenever anyone but a returning team nears the ramp.']),
 ('sec', 'What is the one known thing that can pass straight through a closed iris?',
  'Tollan phasing technology passes through solid matter, so it is the only thing known to breach the iris.',
  ['A fast enough projectile can pass through, which is why the iris is built from many layered plates.',
   'A beam from a staff weapon passes through, since the iris is only designed to stop solid objects.',
   'Nothing can breach it, because the plates are fused together tightly once the iris has fully closed.']),
 ('malp', 'What does a MALP report to the team before they go through?',
  'It measures temperature and oxygen, and shows whether a dial-home device is there so the team can get home.',
  ['It reports how many people are on the far side and what kind of weapons they are carrying at the time.',
   'It maps the whole planet in a few seconds and sends back a full survey of every region and every ocean.',
   'It reads the far gate\'s symbols and sends back a list of every address that gate has ever dialed out to.']),
 ('malp', 'Why does a MALP roll instead of fly?',
  'It is a ground rover on treads or heavy wheels, built to survey the ground near the gate and send video back.',
  ['It hovers on small thrusters, because rolling along the ground would set off traps buried near the gate.',
   'It is carried by the team, and the wheels are only there to make it easier to hand from one person to another.',
   'It drifts through the wormhole and sinks to the ground, so it cannot be steered once it has arrived.']),
 ('malp', 'Why might a MALP\'s picture not be the whole truth?',
  'A hostile world can tamper with the signal and feed a false image through the relay, so a second look is wise.',
  ['A MALP camera sees only a narrow band of colors, so what the team sees is always slightly different from reality.',
   'The wormhole bends the image on the way back, so everything arrives reversed from the original scene.',
   'A MALP adds a built-in delay to its video, so whatever the team is watching is always about an hour old.']),
 ('malp', 'Why does the gate room have such a long ramp?',
  'It lets vehicles such as a MALP roll through the gate as easily as a person can walk through it.',
  ['The ramp slows the wormhole down, so a traveler arriving at speed is brought gently to a stop on the far side.',
   'It holds the gate\'s power cables, which would otherwise be a tripping hazard across the gate room floor.',
   'The iris sits on the ramp, so it needs to be long enough to fit every plate when the iris fully opens.']),
 ('arm', 'Why does a zat need more than one shot?',
  'One shot stuns with intense pain, two usually kill, and a third disintegrates the target.',
  ['One shot kills on the spot, and every extra shot just repeats the same damage on the same target again.',
   'The first shot charges the weapon and the second fires it, so a single pull of the trigger does nothing.',
   'Each shot hits harder at range, so the number of shots needed depends on how far away the target stands.']),
 ('arm', 'What does a zat blast do to electronics?',
  'It shorts out most electrical devices, which is why it is a poor weapon to use near delicate equipment.',
  ['It supercharges them, so an electronic device hit by a zat works better for several minutes afterward.',
   'It does nothing to electronics at all, because the energy is only ever absorbed by living tissue.',
   'It wipes the memory of a device but leaves its circuits untouched and ready to be loaded again.']),
 ('arm', 'What is the catch with a sarcophagus?',
  'It heals nearly any wound or illness, but regular use can bring addiction and a swollen ego.',
  ['It heals only broken bones, and every other injury still has to be treated by the normal medical staff.',
   'It works only once for each person, and a second session is certain to be fatal to the patient.',
   'It has no side effects whatever, which is exactly why the Goa\'uld use one every single day.']),
 ('arm', 'How are transport rings different from the gate?',
  'Rings move people and cargo over short distances, such as ground to orbit, and work both ways.',
  ['Rings open a wormhole to any gate on the network, and they are simply a smaller version of the stargate.',
   'Rings only work in a straight line across open ground, and they cannot be used from inside a building.',
   'Rings send matter only upward from the ground to a ship, and a separate device brings it back down again.']),
 ('arm', 'Why can rings fail to reach a team buried deep underground?',
  'Rings cannot punch through a great thickness of solid material, such as a mile of ice, without modification.',
  ['Rings only work at night, since the energy they use is gathered from the sky during the hours of daylight.',
   'Rings can only carry a certain weight, and a team with its full gear is always over the limit.',
   'Rings need a clear view of the sky to lock on, and the roof of any building will block the signal.']),
 ('arm', 'Why can a death glider not jump to hyperspace?',
  'It is too small to carry the generator that opens a hyperspace window, so only bigger ships have hyperdrives.',
  ['A glider is built from naquadah, and that material cannot be used anywhere near a working hyperdrive.',
   'A glider has no navigator, so the hyperdrive would have no one to point it in the right direction.',
   'Hyperspace can only be entered through a stargate, so a glider has to be carried through one first.']),
]
